# Apple push for the relay: a systemd drop-in, installed by hand as
# /etc/systemd/system/hide-relay.service.d/apns.conf (docs/RELAY_PROTOCOL.md,
# Push relay). Without it the relay runs as before and answers every push
# request `unavailable`.
#
# The key is Apple's .p8 and the relay's only secret. It lives on the relay
# host only, never in the repository, CI, an environment variable or a log:
#   install -d -m 0700 -o root -g root /etc/hide-relay
#   install -m 0400 -o root -g root AuthKey_<key id>.p8 /etc/hide-relay/apns.p8
# systemd copies it into the service's private credential directory (%d), a
# root-owned 0400 file the service user reads through a POSIX ACL; with an ACL
# entry the group bits of the mode show the ACL mask, so the relay sees 0440.
# The relay therefore allows group read and refuses to start when the file has
# any bit for others, a group write bit, or cannot be read.
#
# The key id and team id are Apple's, not secret; the private operations
# document holds the values. The topic is the iOS app's bundle id. Both of
# Apple's hosts are used: each request names the environment its phone was
# issued in (sandbox for a development build, production for TestFlight and the
# App Store), and the relay picks api.sandbox.push.apple.com or
# api.push.apple.com from it. No --apns-endpoint here: that names a loopback
# stand-in for tests only.
#
# ExecStart repeats deploy/relay/hide-relay.service's line with the four Apple
# arguments added; change both together.
[Service]
LoadCredential=apns.p8:/etc/hide-relay/apns.p8
ExecStart=
ExecStart=/opt/hide-relay/current/hide-relay --listen 127.0.0.1:8787 --trusted-proxy 127.0.0.1 --apns-key %d/apns.p8 --apns-key-id <key id> --apns-team-id <team id> --apns-topic me.grab.hide.ios
